    "slug": "healthy-loans-to-deposits-ratio",
    "question": "What is a healthy loans-to-deposits ratio for a bank?",
    "cluster": "efficiency",
    "clusterName": "Bank Efficiency and Funding",
    "intentType": "evaluative",
    "shortAnswer": "A healthy loans-to-deposits ratio for most banks falls between 70% and 90%. A ratio in this range means the bank is actively lending its deposit base without overextending into less stable funding sources or leaving too much money sitting idle in lower-yielding investments.",
    "fullAnswer": "The loans-to-deposits ratio (LTD) measures how much of a bank's deposit base has been lent out. The calculation is simple: divide total loans by total deposits. An 80% ratio means the bank has lent 80 cents of every dollar deposited, with the rest held in securities, cash, or other liquid assets.\n\nMost U.S. banks operate best somewhere between 70% and 90%. The logic behind this range is straightforward: banks earn money by lending deposits at higher rates than they pay depositors, so a ratio too far below 70% means the bank isn't fully using its cheapest funding source. But loans are illiquid. A bank can't quickly convert them to cash if depositors want their money back, so pushing too far above 90% leaves thin liquidity cushions. Once the ratio crosses 100%, the bank has started borrowing from more expensive outside sources to fund its lending.\n\n## What Low Ratios Signal\n\nA ratio below 70% usually points to one of a few situations:\n\n- Loan demand in the bank's local markets is weak, leaving fewer creditworthy borrowers to lend to\n\n- The bank recently dealt with credit losses and has pulled back on new lending while it rebuilds loan quality\n\n- Management is holding extra liquidity as a defensive measure during uncertain economic conditions\n\nNone of these are necessarily red flags on their own. The real concern is profitability. Securities and cash earn less than loans, so a bank sitting on deposits it can't lend profitably will post lower net interest margins and weaker returns on assets. Over time, a persistently low LTD ratio raises questions about whether the bank can generate enough loan volume to support its cost structure.\n\n## What High Ratios Signal\n\nAbove 90%, the bank has less room to absorb unexpected deposit outflows without selling assets or tapping borrowing lines. Above 100%, part of the loan portfolio is being funded with wholesale sources like Federal Home Loan Bank (FHLB) advances, brokered deposits, or repurchase agreements.\n\nWholesale funding isn't inherently dangerous. Many well-run banks use it strategically during periods of strong loan growth. The issue is cost and stability. Wholesale funding almost always costs more than core customer deposits, which compresses net interest margin. And unlike a local depositor's checking account, wholesale funding can vanish quickly. During the 2008 financial crisis, banks that leaned heavily on wholesale markets found those funding channels frozen, creating severe liquidity stress even at institutions whose loan portfolios were performing well.\n\n## How Bank Type Affects the Range\n\nA single \"healthy\" range doesn't capture the full picture because different kinds of banks naturally run at different levels.\n\nCommunity banks in rural or slow-growth areas often operate with ratios in the 65-80% range. They collect more deposits than they can lend locally and invest the surplus in securities. This is normal for their business model, though it limits earnings upside.\n\nRegional banks with active commercial lending programs tend to run higher, often 80-95%. They have broader geographic reach, more lending opportunities, and more sophisticated treasury operations to manage their funding mix.\n\nLarge national banks show the widest variation. Some hold lean loan books relative to their massive deposit bases, while others lend aggressively and supplement with wholesale funding. Comparing a large money-center bank's ratio to a community bank's tells you very little. Size and business mix need to match for any comparison to be useful.\n\n## Putting the Number in Context\n\nThe absolute ratio matters less than three things: the trend, the peer group, and what's behind the number.\n\nA ratio climbing from 75% to 92% over three years shows aggressive loan growth. Whether that's encouraging or concerning depends on whether credit quality has held up alongside the expansion. Rapidly growing loan books can mask loosening underwriting standards for several quarters before problem loans surface.\n\nPeer comparison tells you more than any fixed threshold. A bank at 88% in a market where competitors average 82% is lending more aggressively than its neighbors. That could mean stronger loan origination, or it could mean the bank is reaching for loans that others passed on. The ratio alone doesn't answer that question, but it tells you where to look next.\n\nFinally, look past the ratio to the funding behind it. Two banks can both show a 95% LTD ratio, but one might be funded almost entirely by sticky checking and savings accounts from long-term customers, while the other depends on rate-sensitive CDs and brokered deposits that will leave when rates shift. The quality of the deposits backing the loans matters as much as the quantity.",
